{"date": "2026-11-14", "post_id": 1511402, "title": "Apollo 12 and Surveyor 3", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-14-apollo-12-and-surveyor-3/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Put on your <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anaglyph_3D#/media/File:Anaglyph_glasses.png\">red/blue glasses</a> and gaze across the western <a href=\"https://www.alanbeangallery.com/tiptoeing-on-the-ocean-of-storms\">Ocean of Storms</a> on the surface of the Moon. The 3D <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/earth-matters/2016/11/21/what-on-earth-is-an-anaglyph/\">anaglyph</a> shows Apollo 12 astronaut Pete Conrad visiting the Surveyor 3 spacecraft in November of 1969. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/surveyor-3/\">Surveyor 3 had landed</a>at the site on the inside slope of a small crater about 2 1/2 years earlier in April of 1967. Visible on the horizon beyond the far crater wall, Apollo 12's <a href=\"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1969-099C\">Lunar Module Intrepid</a> touched down less than 200 meters (650 feet) away, easy moonwalking distance from the robotic Surveyor spacecraft. This stereo image was carefully created from two separate pictures (<a href=\"https://www.lpi.usra.edu/resources/apollo/frame/?AS12-48-7133\">AS12-48-7133</a>, <a href=\"https://www.lpi.usra.edu/resources/apollo/frame/?AS12-48-7134\">AS12-48-7134</a>) captured on the <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/moonbase/\">lunar surface</a>. They depict the scene from only slightly different viewpoints, approximating the separation between human eyes.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>meteors through Orion", "credit": "<a href=\"https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/missions/apollo-12/in-depth/\">Apollo 12</a>, <a href=\"https://www.alanbeangallery.com/\">Alan Bean</a> - Stereo Image Copyright: <a href=\"https://www.hq.nasa.gov/alsj/a12/ApAnKF.html\">Kevin Frank</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/missions/apollo-12/in-depth/\">Apollo 12</a>, <a href=\"https://www.alanbeangallery.com/\">Alan Bean</a> - Stereo Image Copyright: <a href=\"https://www.hq.nasa.gov/alsj/a12/ApAnKF.html\">Kevin Frank</a>", "alt": "Astronaut standing next to Surveyor 3 on the Moon.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-14-apollo-12-and-surveyor-3/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/KF-ApAn48-7133-4.jpg?w=2181&h=2200&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 14 - Apollo 12 and Surveyor 3</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-14-apollo-12-and-surveyor-3/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Put on your red/blue glasses and gaze across the western Ocean of Storms on the surface of the Moon. The 3D anaglyph shows Apollo 12 astronaut Pete Conrad visiting the Surveyor 3 spacecraft in November of 1969. Surveyor 3 had landedat the site on the inside slope of a small crater about 2 1/2 years earlier in April of 1967. Visible on the horizon beyond the far crater wall, Apollo 12&#039;s Lunar Module Intrepid touched down less than 200 meters (650 feet) away, easy moonwalking distance from the robotic Surveyor spacecraft. This stereo image was carefully created from two separate pictures (AS12-48-7133, AS12-48-7134) captured on the lunar surface. They depict the scene from only slightly different viewpoints, approximating the separation between human eyes.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: meteors through Orion\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"apollo 12 and surveyor 3\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Apollo 12 and Surveyor 3\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-14-apollo-12-and-surveyor-3/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/KF-ApAn48-7133-4.jpg?w=2181&#038;h=2200&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/KF-ApAn48-7133-4.jpg?w=2181&#038;h=2200&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Astronaut standing next to Surveyor 3 on the Moon.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-14T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-13T12:49:39-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Apollo 12 and Surveyor 3\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/KF-ApAn48-7133-4.jpg?w=2181&#038;h=2200&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 14<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/KF-ApAn48-7133-4.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/KF-ApAn48-7133-4.jpg\" alt=\"Astronaut standing next to Surveyor 3 on the Moon.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Apollo 12 and Surveyor 3 </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/missions/apollo-12/in-depth/\">Apollo 12</a>, <a href=\"https://www.alanbeangallery.com/\">Alan Bean</a> - Stereo Image Copyright: <a href=\"https://www.hq.nasa.gov/alsj/a12/ApAnKF.html\">Kevin Frank</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Put on your <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anaglyph_3D#/media/File:Anaglyph_glasses.png\">red/blue glasses</a> and gaze across the western <a href=\"https://www.alanbeangallery.com/tiptoeing-on-the-ocean-of-storms\">Ocean of Storms</a> on the surface of the Moon. The 3D <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/earth-matters/2016/11/21/what-on-earth-is-an-anaglyph/\">anaglyph</a> shows Apollo 12 astronaut Pete Conrad visiting the Surveyor 3 spacecraft in November of 1969. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/surveyor-3/\">Surveyor 3 had landed</a>at the site on the inside slope of a small crater about 2 1/2 years earlier in April of 1967. Visible on the horizon beyond the far crater wall, Apollo 12's <a href=\"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1969-099C\">Lunar Module Intrepid</a> touched down less than 200 meters (650 feet) away, easy moonwalking distance from the robotic Surveyor spacecraft. This stereo image was carefully created from two separate pictures (<a href=\"https://www.lpi.usra.edu/resources/apollo/frame/?AS12-48-7133\">AS12-48-7133</a>, <a href=\"https://www.lpi.usra.edu/resources/apollo/frame/?AS12-48-7134\">AS12-48-7134</a>) captured on the <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/moonbase/\">lunar surface</a>. They depict the scene from only slightly different viewpoints, approximating the separation between human eyes.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>meteors through Orion\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-13-ngc-206-and-the-star-clouds-of-andromeda/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nJerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261114/html"}